# INVENTION SUMMARY

## Portable Collapsible Safety Display Stand

### Field of the Invention

The present invention relates generally to portable display and signage devices, and more particularly to a collapsible safety display stand that transitions between a flat, compact configuration suitable for storage and transport, and an erected, free-standing box-like configuration suitable for field deployment as a warning, informational, or safety signage stand.

### Background

Safety displays used at construction sites, roadways, event venues, and industrial facilities must often be transported in bulk and deployed quickly. Conventional rigid stands are cumbersome to store and ship due to their fixed three-dimensional shape, while purely flat signage lacks the structural stability, visibility, and weather resistance of an erected stand. There exists a need for a display device that folds flat for efficient storage and shipping, yet reliably and repeatably erects into a stable, self-supporting structure without tools or separate fasteners.

### Summary of the Invention

The invention provides a portable safety display stand (10) fabricated from a foldable sheet material, such as corrugated plastic, coated paperboard, or similar semi-rigid panel stock, which is scored and hinged along a plurality of fold lines to permit collapse into a substantially flat configuration and expansion into an erected, three-dimensional box configuration.

In the erected configuration, the stand (10) comprises:

- A **front wall (12)**, bearing a display surface for safety graphics, warnings, or informational content, the front wall (12) extending upwardly from a base region and defining the primary viewing panel of the device;

- A **lid (14)**, hingedly connected along an upper fold line to an upper edge of the front wall (12) and/or a rear wall panel, the lid (14) being foldable between a closed position overlying an upper opening of the erected box structure and an open position exposing an interior cavity;

- A pair of **leg members (16)**, hingedly connected to opposing side edges of the front wall (12) and/or to side wall panels, each leg member (16) foldable outwardly and downwardly from a collapsed, flush position to a deployed position in which the leg members (16) contact a support surface and elevate and stabilize the front wall (12) in an upright orientation;

- One or more **side wall panels (18)**, extending between the front wall (12) and a rear wall panel (20), the side wall panels (18) folding inwardly during collapse and outwardly during erection to define lateral boundaries of the box structure;

- A **rear wall panel (20)**, hingedly joined to the lid (14) and/or side wall panels (18), cooperating with the front wall (12), side wall panels (18), and lid (14) to define an enclosed or partially enclosed box structure when the stand (10) is in the erected configuration;

- A plurality of **locking tabs and corresponding slots (22, 24)**, disposed along edges of the front wall (12), side wall panels (18), lid (14), and/or leg members (16), configured to interlock upon erection of the stand (10) so as to releasably retain the front wall (12), lid (14), leg members (16), side wall panels (18), and rear wall panel (20) in their deployed positions without the use of separate fasteners, adhesives, or tools;

- A plurality of **fold lines (26)**, comprising scored, perforated, or reduced-thickness regions of the sheet material, disposed between adjacent panels including the front wall (12), lid (14), leg members (16), side wall panels (18), and rear wall panel (20), to facilitate repeated folding and unfolding between the flat and erected configurations without material fatigue or failure.

### Operation

In the flat, collapsed configuration, the front wall (12), lid (14), leg members (16), side wall panels (18), and rear wall panel (20) lie substantially coplanar with one another, joined along the fold lines (26), such that the stand (10) presents a low-profile, stackable form factor suitable for palletized shipping or compact storage.

To erect the stand (10), an operator unfolds the leg members (16) outwardly about their respective fold lines (26) until the locking tabs (22) engage the corresponding slots (24), fixing the leg members (16) in a load-bearing position. The side wall panels (18) and rear wall panel (20) are similarly unfolded and interlocked via tabs and slots (22, 24) to form the box structure, and the lid (14) is folded into its closed or open position as desired, likewise engaging complementary locking features. The resulting structure is a self-supporting, free-standing display stand having a rigid front wall (12) for graphic display, elevated and stabilized by the leg members (16), with an interior cavity accessible via the lid (14) for storage of literature, weights, or ballast to further stabilize the stand (10) against wind or displacement.

### Advantages

The invention provides a lightweight, tool-free, reusable display stand (10) that:

1. Collapses to a flat profile (10) for efficient storage, stacking, and shipping;
2. Erects quickly into a stable, self-supporting box structure via integrated fold lines (26) and locking tabs/slots (22, 24), without separate hardware;
3. Provides a dedicated display surface via the front wall (12) while offering enclosed storage via the lid (14) and interior cavity;
4. Employs leg members (16) to elevate and visually orient the display for improved visibility and stability;
5. Is suitable for manufacture from recyclable or weather-resistant sheet materials at low cost.

### Alternative Embodiments

It will be appreciated that the number, arrangement, and configuration of side wall panels (18), leg members (16), and locking tab/slot pairs (22, 24) may be varied without departing from the scope of the invention. Additional embodiments may incorporate reinforcing ribs, ballast pockets, handle apertures, or reflective/retroreflective sheeting affixed to the front wall (12) to enhance visibility in low-light conditions. The lid (14) may further include a secondary locking flap or window aperture to permit viewing of stored contents without full erection of the stand (10).
